n" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500261 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500261 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c7bb68ec-1f3f-5057-8cda-d0404350a2d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500269 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500269 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500269 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500269 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500269 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500269 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500269 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500269 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500269 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da96cd01-655c-5aad-b1ac-38d2723dadfa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500275 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500275 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500275 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500275 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500275 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500275 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500275 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500275 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500275 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ede57a7a-8e37-5d0b-866a-4f7edf2e1203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500286 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500286 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159646" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500286 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500286 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=14$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=16$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=9$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=7,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500286 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "157efc5d-8a57-58a8-ada2-5fc55f4e29c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500286 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=9-7=2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3}^{2}}{14}+\\frac{{5}^{2}}{16}}≈2.911\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2\\pm 2.911\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500301 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500301 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159639" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500301 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500301 #edb/ref 1071 "It is known that the weights of oranges from farms X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=7$ grams and ${\\sigma }_{y}=8$ grams, respectively. The owner of the farms wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the weights of oranges from farm X is smaller than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the weights of oranges from farm Y. He decides to construct a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe farmer sampled $25$ oranges from farm X and $20$ oranges from farm Y, finding that their mean weights are $125$ grams and $128.2$ grams, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-3.2\\pm 3.737.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500301 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "394c3561-19e2-5323-928c-73d8d3b37d89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500301 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=125-128.2=-3.2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{8}^{2}}{20}}≈3.737\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3.2\\pm 3.737\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. 