he population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=15-16=-1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{10}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{9}^{2}}{21}}≈7.221\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\pm 7.221\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500368 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500368 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500368 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500368 #edb/ref 1071 "It is known that sales (quantities) of a new product in two different states X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=7$, respectively. A marketing agency wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of sales in state X is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of sales in state Y. They decide to construct a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the two population means.\n\nThe agency sampled $14$ shops from state X and $15$ shops from state Y, finding that their mean sales were $54.5$ and $48.6$, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are 